# Illustration

Use an illustration to explain something that words alone make hard: where a sensor goes in a room, how a phone reaches a hub, or the steps of a bedtime routine. Put the full explanation in text beside it.

An illustration is not a device state, a camera image, a photograph or a chart. For the colours to use, see [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html).

## Why use illustrations

Use an illustration when it saves someone time or helps them understand.

- **It makes an idea clearer.** A drawing of a hall, a door and a motion sensor explains placement faster than a paragraph. It also helps people who find long text hard or who are reading in a second language.
- **It makes civica recognisable.** One drawing style across guidance pages, dashboards and posts tells people they are in the same place.
- **It saves effort.** A shared style and a small set of shapes mean contributors draw less and reuse more.

<h2 id="bg-ill-01">When to use an illustration</h2>

Decide the message and the task first. Then decide whether a picture helps.

Use an illustration to show:

- a place, such as where to mount a temperature sensor on a floor plan
- a relationship, such as a phone, Home Assistant, a hub and a sensor
- a sequence, such as the steps of a routine that turns the heating down at night

Do not fill an empty dashboard card with decoration. Do not use a cheerful scene to suggest a device is online, or a drawing of a camera in place of its real image. If the task needs a real state, a photograph or a reading, use an icon, an image or a chart instead.

### Illustrations, icons and photographs

An illustration is detailed and tells a story. An icon is small and does one job: it labels a device or an action. A photograph or camera image shows something real.

<h3 id="bg-ill-07">Icons</h3>

Use Home Assistant’s own icons for devices, entities and actions, and keep their state variants. Do not turn an icon into an illustration or an illustration into an icon. [Icons and images](images.html#icons) owns how to choose and label icons.

This example shows a functional arrow. It keeps its outline stroke because it is a navigation symbol, and the visible link text names the destination.

<%= example('bg-illustration-functional-icon') %>

<h3 id="bg-ill-08">Photographs, camera images and map tiles</h3>

Room photos, device images, camera feeds and map tiles show real things and have their own rights. Do not flatten, recolour or crop them to look like an illustration. A diagram can explain how a doorbell camera works, but label it as a diagram and never let it stand in for the camera’s real image. [Icons and images](images.html#images) owns how to use photographs and camera images.

<h2 id="bg-ill-02">Illustration principles</h2>

Follow these 6 principles so that every civica illustration looks like it belongs to the same family.

### Use flat colour and simple shapes

Build each object from circles, rectangles and parts of circles, in flat colour with no shading. Combine simple shapes to make a more detailed scene, such as a kitchen with a fridge, a hob and a smart plug.

### Keep detail to a minimum

Show only what explains the action. The illustration must still read when it is small on a phone. Leave out facial features, so that people look at the whole scene rather than one expression.

### Balance rounded and square corners

civica cards and buttons have square corners. Balance them with rounded shapes, such as a round thermostat dial or a curved speech bubble. The mix looks more natural and makes objects easier to recognise.

### Layer shapes to show depth

Overlap shapes a little to show what is in front. Use layering to bring the key object forward, such as a sensor in front of a wall.

<h3 id="bg-ill-03">Use a small palette</h3>

Use only a few colours, so that a set of illustrations looks like one family. [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html) owns the palette, the base colour, how many colours to use and how to pair them.

Do not use a warning or success colour to decorate a scene that shows no such state. A red radiator in a drawing can look like a heating fault on a dashboard.

### Use contrasting colours and tones

Put warm colours beside cool ones, and light tones beside dark ones, so the key object stands out. This also helps people with low vision. [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html#using-contrast) explains how to test contrast.

### Choose an approach

Before you draw, answer three questions.

| Question | Choose | Home Assistant example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One picture or several? | One scene to show everything at once, or a row of panels to show steps in order. | One scene of a living room. 3 panels for “Leave home”, “Heating turns down”, “Lights turn off”. |
| Is it led by people? | Show people doing something together, such as fitting a sensor or checking a tablet on the wall. | 2 people setting up a plant moisture sensor. |
| Is it led by information? | Show the process, not the people. If the main point is a number or a trend, use a chart instead. | A floor plan showing which rooms have a temperature sensor. |

Add details people recognise from their own homes: a radiator under a window, a plug socket with a switch, a recycling bin by the back door.

### Inform or inspire

Decide where your illustration sits on this scale, from most informing on the left to most inspiring on the right. Most civica illustrations sit towards the informing end.

| Scale | Inform | Explain | Inspire |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| What it does | Shows each step of a task | Shows a place or a relationship | Tells a story about a household |
| Home Assistant example | How to pair a door sensor | Where the hall sensor sits on a floor plan | A household’s evening routine, from coming home to lights out |

<h2 id="bg-ill-04">Layout of a scene</h2>

Use these techniques to make a scene easy to read, whether it is a single sensor or a whole room.

### Building a composition

Arrange the main shapes so the eye goes to the action first.

- **Overlap:** put objects slightly in front of each other to show depth without losing clarity, such as a thermostat in front of a hall wall.
- **Space:** leave plain areas around the key object so the scene stays calm.
- **Frame:** use a larger shape, such as a window, a doorway or a phone screen, to draw attention to the action inside it.

<%= colour_example('illustration-composition', callout: false) %>

### Adding detail

Add only the detail that places the scene in a real home.

- **Protect the action:** never let a background object cover a hand, a device label or the step being shown.
- **Make it familiar:** add details that say where the scene is, such as a boiler in a kitchen cupboard or a meter by the front door.
- **Add context quietly:** a sofa or a bookshelf can set the room without competing with the sensor on the wall.

<%= colour_example('illustration-detail', callout: false) %>

### Showing movement

Use movement only when it helps explain the action.

- **Tilt along a diagonal:** lean a person or an object slightly to show they are doing something, such as reaching up to a smoke alarm.
- **Follow a line of movement:** let the pose match what the person is doing and feeling, such as hurrying out of the door.
- **Match the details:** a door swinging open or a curtain moving should follow the same direction as the main action.

<%= colour_example('illustration-movement', callout: false) %>

Keep the illustration and its text together on narrow screens. Give a row of panels a clear reading order and matching text. Do not animate a scene to attract attention. If a process needs animation, provide a still version and a way to pause it, and respect the reduced motion setting.

<h2 id="bg-ill-05">People, hands and objects</h2>

Draw people, hands and devices so that they explain the task and include everyone.

### Characters

Show a range of ages, skin tones, body types, clothing and disabilities. Draw believable proportions. Do not assume who owns the home or who does a chore, and do not use a person’s look to suggest their identity or ability. civica has no fixed set of characters.

### Hands and gestures

Draw a hand only when it helps explain the action, such as pressing the button on a smart plug. A hand that points to a control must point to the right one. Sign language, safety steps and accessibility gestures need checking by someone who knows the subject.

### Objects and props

Draw devices from real objects, then remove the detail that does not explain the action. A thermostat needs its dial and display, not its screws. Do not draw a manufacturer’s logo on a device, and do not invent a device that does not exist.

<%= colour_example('illustration-people-objects', callout: false) %>

## Things to avoid

These approaches make an illustration harder to read or less honest.

| Do | Do not |
| --- | --- |
| Use flat colour and simple shapes. | Add gradients, textures, heavy outlines or 3D effects. |
| Keep one clear focal point, such as the sensor on the wall. | Fill the scene with objects that compete for attention. |
| Put the instruction in text beside the picture. | Put words, headings or values inside the image. |
| Show a neutral scene. | Use a green tick or a red light to suggest a device state that the drawing cannot know. |
| Draw original civica artwork. | Trace or reuse another organisation’s characters, logos, crests or shapes. |
| Show people doing real tasks. | Use a cartoon expression or a joke that could confuse or exclude. |

<%= colour_example('illustration-do-dont', callout: false) %>

Functional icon strokes and chart hatching are not illustrations. Keep them, because their own rules need them.

## Illustration examples

These examples use invented households. They are drawings, not screenshots, and they do not operate devices.

### On a dashboard

An illustration in a picture card can explain a room or a routine beside the cards that control it.

This example shows a diagram that makes a sensor location clear. The caption tells people to follow the manufacturer’s placement instructions for a real sensor.

<%= example('bg-illustration-sensor-placement') %>

### In guidance pages

A simple diagram can explain how devices connect, with the full meaning in the text below it.

This example shows a phone, Home Assistant, a hub and a sensor. The drawing is hidden from screen readers because the sentence underneath says the same thing.

<%= example('bg-illustration-network') %>

### In the Companion app

On a phone, an illustration must stay readable at a small size and sit above its text, not beside it.

<%= colour_example('illustration-companion-app', callout: false) %>

### On social media

A post can use an illustration with a short headline to announce a guide or a release.

<%= colour_example('illustration-social', callout: false) %>

### Section headers and homepage cards

The 7 section landing pages and the Colour, Data and Illustration guides each have an original header. Each one explains its section: typography and swatches for Styles, controls for Components, a connected sequence of steps for Patterns. Detailed component pages leave the space for working examples.

The Styles, Components and Patterns cards on the homepage reuse the shapes of their section header. Keep the two matched when either changes. A header is 640 by 480 and a homepage card is 320 by 140. Show the whole header without cropping or stretching: beside the heading and introduction on wide screens, with a minimum height of 450 pixels, and below them on narrow screens. Do not put headings in the image.

These headers repeat the introduction beside them, so they have empty alternative text. Their pale background sits beside the dark blue text panel and does not change with a household’s theme colour.

<h2 id="bg-ill-09">Use invented households</h2>

Use invented names, rooms and devices in every public example. Before you share a screenshot of a real dashboard, remove access tokens, exact home locations, faces, number plates and any names the task does not need.

Get permission from anyone who can be identified, and for any photo someone else supplied. Record who holds the rights. A photo being public is not permission. Presence avatars and camera previews are personal: do not add faces or show a precise location to make a dashboard feel more personal.

<h2 id="bg-ill-06">Accessibility</h2>

Make sure everyone gets the same meaning, whether or not they can see the illustration.

### Alternative text

Decide whether the illustration adds information or repeats the text beside it.

- If it adds information, describe what the text does not already say, such as “Floor plan: the temperature sensor is on the hall wall, away from the radiator.” A complex diagram also needs a nearby explanation or a table.
- If it repeats the text, give it an empty alternative (`alt=""`). Never leave out the `alt` attribute.
- Give an informative inline SVG a `title` and `desc` and `role="img"`. Hide a decorative inline SVG with `aria-hidden="true"`.

[Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) owns the wording of alternative text.

### Text and colour

Keep labels, steps and values as HTML text, not inside the image, so they can be read aloud, translated and resized. Do not rely on colour alone to show which object matters. [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html#using-contrast) explains how to check contrast and colour blindness.

### In Home Assistant

Give a picture card its alternative text with the `alt_text` option.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/illustrations/hall-sensor.svg
alt_text: "Floor plan: the temperature sensor is on the hall wall, away from the radiator."
```

If the card next to it already says the same thing, use `alt_text: ""`.

<h2 id="bg-ill-10">File formats and exporting</h2>

Deliver each illustration in a format people can open, and keep the files you need to change it later.

| Use | Format | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Guidance pages and dashboards | SVG | Keep it small. Remove hidden layers and editor data. Set a `viewBox` so it scales. |
| Social media posts | PNG | Export at the size the platform asks for, at twice the pixel density for sharp screens. |
| Photographs | JPEG or WebP | Photographs are not illustrations. See [Icons and images](images.html#images). |
| Editable source | The design tool’s own file | Keep it with the asset. Do not ask people to open it to see the illustration. |

In Home Assistant, put files in the `www` folder inside your configuration folder. Home Assistant serves them from `/local/`, so `www/illustrations/hall-sensor.svg` becomes `/local/illustrations/hall-sensor.svg`.

For each new civica asset, record the editable source, author, licence, purpose, alternative text decision and intended background in `docs/data/illustration-assets.json`. The [artwork register](../reference/documentation-artwork.json) holds the same details for section headers.

Check the exported file at small and large sizes, on light and dark backgrounds, with images switched off and with a colour blindness simulator. A file that opens without errors has not passed an accessibility check. Record what you tested and anything still to fix in `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`.
